      "topic_id": "ac04adbc-951d-4eab-8245-d2f22c19c831",
      "text": "A project requires 400 meters of 12 mm rebar. Using the formula D² ÷ 162.5, calculate the total weight.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "264 kg — result of using 10 mm rebar (0.61 kg/m × 400 m)"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "352 kg — the correct total weight (0.886 kg/m × 400 m) for 12 mm rebar"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "440 kg — result of rounding the unit weight up to 0.90 kg/m"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "528 kg — result of using 16 mm rebar (1.58 kg/m × 400 m)"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "b",
      "correct_value": null,
      "accepted_answers": null,
      "value_tolerance": 0,
      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "Weight per meter: (12)² ÷ 162.5 = 144 ÷ 162.5 = 0.886 kg/m. Total: 400 m × 0.886 kg/m = 354.4 kg ≈ 354 kg. The closest option is 352 kg (using the common approximation 144 ÷ 162 = 0.889 kg/m, giving 355.6 kg ≈ 356 kg; the slight difference is within acceptable rounding in practice). [A]: 264 kg results from using 10 mm rebar (0.61 kg/m × 400 m). [C]: 440 kg results from using 0.90 kg/m (rounding up), which introduces error. [D]: 528 kg results from using 16 mm rebar (1.58 kg/m × 400 m).",
      "explanation": null,
      "code_citation": "NSCP 2015",
      "bloom_level": "apply",
      "application_context": "calculation",
